2025-08-12 16:46:07,881 - root - DEBUG - Sefaria's Search API response: {"took": 89, "timed_out": false, "_shards": {"total": 1, "successful": 1, "skipped": 0, "failed": 0}, "hits": {"total": 10000, "max_score": 4966663.0, "hits": [{"_index": "text-a", "_id": "Pirkei Avot 5:6 (Sefaria Community Translation [en])", "_score": 4966663.0, "_source": {"ref": "Pirkei Avot 5:6", "heRef": "משנה אבות ה׳:ו׳", "version": "Sefaria Community Translation", "lang": "en", "version_priority": 4, "titleVariants": ["Avot", "M Avot", "Mishna Avot", "M. Avot", "Avoth", "Perkei Avot", "Mishnah Avot", "Pirke Abot", "Ethics of the Fathers", "<i>Ethics of the Fathers</i>", "<i>Pirkei Avot</i>", "Pir. Av.", "Ab.", "Aboth", "Mishnah Ab.", "Mishnah Aboth", "Pir. Ab.", "Avos", "Pirkei Avos", "Pirkei Avot"], "categories": ["Mishnah", "Seder Nezikin"], "order": "A00100300800050006", "path": "Mishnah/Seder Nezikin/Pirkei Avot", "pagesheetrank": 626287.286920008, "comp_date": 190, "exact": "Ten things were created on the eve of the [first] Shabbat at twilight. And these are they: The mouth of the earth [that swallowed Korach in Numbers 16:32]; and the mouth of the well [that accompanied the Israelites in the wilderness in Numbers 21:17]; and the mouth of the donkey [that spoke to Bilaam in Numbers 22:28–30]; and the rainbow [that served as a covenant after the flood in Genesis 9:13]; and the manna [that God provided the Israelites in the wilderness in Exodus 16:4–21]; and the staff [of Moshe]; and the shamir ; and the letters; and the writing; and the tablets [all of the latter three, of the Ten Commandments]. And some say, also the destructive spirits, and the burial place of Moshe, our teacher, and the ram of Abraham, our father. And some say, also the [first human-made] tongs, made with [Divine] tongs.", "naive_lemmatizer": "Ten things were created on the eve of the [first] Shabbat at twilight. And these are they: The mouth of the earth [that swallowed Korach in Numbers 16:32]; and the mouth of the well [that accompanied the Israelites in the wilderness in Numbers 21:17]; and the mouth of the donkey [that spoke to Bilaam in Numbers 22:28–30]; and the rainbow [that served as a covenant after the flood in Genesis 9:13]; and the manna [that God provided the Israelites in the wilderness in Exodus 16:4–21]; and the staff [of Moshe]; and the shamir ; and the letters; and the writing; and the tablets [all of the latter three, of the Ten Commandments]. And some say, also the destructive spirits, and the burial place of Moshe, our teacher, and the ram of Abraham, our father. And some say, also the [first human-made] tongs, made with [Divine] tongs.", "hebrew_version_title": "תרגום קהילת ספריא", "languageFamilyName": "english", "isPrimary": false}, "highlight": {"naive_lemmatizer": ["Ten things were created on the eve of the [first] <b>Shabbat</b> at twilight."]}}, {"_index": "text-a", "_id": "Mishneh Torah, Repentance 2:1 (Mishnah Torah, Yod ha-hazakah, trans. by Simon Glazer, 1927 [en])", "_score": 4365629.0, "_source": {"ref": "Mishneh Torah, Repentance 2:1", "heRef": "משנה תורה, הלכות תשובה ב׳:א׳", "version": "Mishnah Torah, Yod ha-hazakah, trans. by Simon Glazer, 1927", "lang": "en", "version_priority": 1, "titleVariants": ["Mishneh Torah, Laws of Repentance", "Mishneh Torah, Teshuvah", "Rambam, Laws of Repentance", "Rambam, Repentance", "Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah", "Rambam, Teshuvah", "Rambam, Hilchot Teshuvah", "Mishneh Torah, Hilkhot Teshuvah", "Rambam, Hilkhot Teshuvah", "Hilkhot Teshuva", "Hilchot Teshuva", "Rambam, Hilkhot Teshuva", "Rambam, Hilchot Teshuva", "Mishneh Torah, Hilkhot Teshuva", "Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuva", "Maimonides, Hilkhoth Teshubah", "Mishneh Torah, Repentance"], "categories": ["Halakhah", "Mishneh Torah", "Sefer Madda"], "order": "A00400000100400020001", "path": "Halakhah/Mishneh Torah/Sefer Madda/Mishneh Torah, Repentance", "pagesheetrank": 634085.7992167557, "comp_date": 1176, "exact": "What is complete repentance? He who once more had in it in his power to repeat a violation, but separated himself therefrom, and did not do it because of repentance, not out of fear or lack of strength. For example? One who knew a woman sinfully, and after a process of time he met her again privately, and he still loving her as theretofore, and he being in a state of potency, and the meeting is in the same land where the sin was first committed, if he parted without sinning, he has attained complete repentance. Of such Solomon said: \"Remember then thy Creator in the days of thy youth\" . Even if he made no reparation save in his old age, at a time when it was already impossible for him to repeat his misdeeds, although it is not the best repentance, it still is of help to him and he is considered a penitent. Moreover, though he continued a life of sin but did repent on his dying day, and did die a penitent, all of his sins are forgiven, even as it is said: \"While the sun, or the light, or the moon, or the stars, be not darkened, nor the clouds return after the rain\" , yea, that is the day of death. Deduct herefrom that if he remembered his Creator and did repent ere he died, he was forgiven. 1 Ibid. 86b; Kiddushin, 40b; Shabbat, 151a. C.", "naive_lemmatizer": "What is complete repentance? He who once more had in it in his power to repeat a violation, but separated himself therefrom, and did not do it because of repentance, not out of fear or lack of strength. For example? One who knew a woman sinfully, and after a process of time he met her again privately, and he still loving her as theretofore, and he being in a state of potency, and the meeting is in the same land where the sin was first committed, if he parted without sinning, he has attained complete repentance. Of such Solomon said: \"Remember then thy Creator in the days of thy youth\" . Even if he made no reparation save in his old age, at a time when it was already impossible for him to repeat his misdeeds, although it is not the best repentance, it still is of help to him and he is considered a penitent. Moreover, though he continued a life of sin but did repent on his dying day, and did die a penitent, all of his sins are forgiven, even as it is said: \"While the sun, or the light, or the moon, or the stars, be not darkened, nor the clouds return after the rain\" , yea, that is the day of death. Deduct herefrom that if he remembered his Creator and did repent ere he died, he was forgiven. 1 Ibid. 86b; Kiddushin, 40b; Shabbat, 151a. C.", "hebrew_version_title": "משנה תורה, יד החזקה, תורגם ע״י סיימון גלייזר, 1927", "languageFamilyName": "english", "isPrimary": false}, "highlight": {"naive_lemmatizer": ["Deduct herefrom that if he remembered his Creator and did repent ere he died, he was forgiven. 1 Ibid. 86b; Kiddushin, 40b; <b>Shabbat</b>, 151a. C."]}}, {"_index": "text-a", "_id": "Exodus 19:17 (The Kehot Chumash; Chabad House Publications, Los Angeles [en])", "_score": 4070585.5, "_source": {"ref": "Exodus 19:17", "heRef": "שמות י״ט:י״ז", "version": "The Kehot Chumash; Chabad House Publications, Los Angeles", "lang": "en", "version_priority": 4, "titleVariants": ["Exod.", "Ex.", "Exo", "Shmot", "Shemot", "Ex", "Exo.", "Exod", "Shemoth", "Shemos", "Exodus"], "categories": ["Tanakh", "Torah"], "order": "A00000000100190017", "path": "Tanakh/Torah/Exodus", "pagesheetrank": 513293.5238936728, "comp_date": -1400, "exact": "God’s presence was palpably coming from all four directions toward the people, in order not to overwhelm them with the intensity of a direct revelation from only one direction. Nonetheless, His presence was felt approaching chiefly from Mount Sinai, accompanied by a retinue of some of His angels, like a bridegroom going to greet his bride. In response, Moses led the people out of the camp toward the Divine Presence, and they took their places at the foot of the mountain. God uprooted the mountain and suspended it over the people, threatening to crush them under it if they would not accept the Torah. But the people accepted this compulsion willingly by lovingly crowding under the mountain. Seeing this, God had the people retreat and lowered the mountain back to the ground. 79 Rashi on Deuteronomy 32:11. 80 Rashi here and on Deuteronomy 33:2. 81 Rashi on Deuteronomy 33:3 and Shabbat 88a.", "naive_lemmatizer": "God’s presence was palpably coming from all four directions toward the people, in order not to overwhelm them with the intensity of a direct revelation from only one direction. Nonetheless, His presence was felt approaching chiefly from Mount Sinai, accompanied by a retinue of some of His angels, like a bridegroom going to greet his bride. In response, Moses led the people out of the camp toward the Divine Presence, and they took their places at the foot of the mountain. God uprooted the mountain and suspended it over the people, threatening to crush them under it if they would not accept the Torah. But the people accepted this compulsion willingly by lovingly crowding under the mountain. Seeing this, God had the people retreat and lowered the mountain back to the ground. 79 Rashi on Deuteronomy 32:11. 80 Rashi here and on Deuteronomy 33:2. 81 Rashi on Deuteronomy 33:3 and Shabbat 88a.", "hebrew_version_title": null, "languageFamilyName": "english", "isPrimary": false}, "highlight": {"naive_lemmatizer": ["Seeing this, God had the people retreat and lowered the mountain back to the ground. 79 Rashi on Deuteronomy 32:11. 80 Rashi here and on Deuteronomy 33:2. 81 Rashi on Deuteronomy 33:3 and <b>Shabbat</b> 88a."]}}, {"_index": "text-a", "_id": "Mishnah Shabbat 7:2 (William Davidson Edition - English [en])", "_score": 2242800.8, "_source": {"ref": "Mishnah Shabbat 7:2", "heRef": "משנה שבת ז׳:ב׳", "version": "William Davidson Edition - English", "lang": "en", "version_priority": 0, "titleVariants": ["M Shabbat", "M. Shabbat", "Mishna Shabbat", "Mishnah Shab.", "Mishnah Sabb.", "Mishnah Sabbath", "Mishnah Shabbat"], "categories": ["Mishnah", "Seder Moed"], "order": "A00100100000070002", "path": "Mishnah/Seder Moed/Mishnah Shabbat", "pagesheetrank": 385875.0422632018, "comp_date": 190, "exact": "This fundamental mishna enumerates those who perform the primary categories of labor prohibited on Shabbat, which number forty-less-one. They are grouped in accordance with their function: One who sows, and one who plows, and one who reaps, and one who gathers sheaves into a pile, and one who threshes, removing the kernel from the husk, and one who winnows threshed grain in the wind, and one who selects the inedible waste from the edible, and one who grinds, and one who sifts the flour in a sieve, and one who kneads dough, and one who bakes. Additional primary categories of prohibited labor are the following: One who shears wool, and one who whitens it, and one who combs the fleece and straightens it, and one who dyes it, and one who spins the wool, and one who stretches the threads of the warp in the loom, and one who constructs two meshes, tying the threads of the warp to the base of the loom, and one who weaves two threads, and one who severs two threads for constructive purposes, and one who ties a knot, and one who unties a knot, and one who sews two stitches with a needle, as well as one who tears a fabric in order to sew two stitches. One who traps a deer, or any living creature, and one who slaughters it, and one who flays it, and one who salts its hide, a step in the tanning process, and one who tans its hide, and one who smooths it, removing hairs and veins, and one who cuts it into measured parts. One who writes two letters and one who erases in order to write two letters. One who builds a structure, and one who dismantles it, one who extinguishes a fire, and one who kindles a fire. One who strikes a blow with a hammer to complete the production process of a vessel , and one who carries out an object from domain to domain. All these are primary categories of labor, and they number forty-less-one. ", "naive_lemmatizer": "This fundamental mishna enumerates those who perform the primary categories of labor prohibited on Shabbat, which number forty-less-one. They are grouped in accordance with their function: One who sows, and one who plows, and one who reaps, and one who gathers sheaves into a pile, and one who threshes, removing the kernel from the husk, and one who winnows threshed grain in the wind, and one who selects the inedible waste from the edible, and one who grinds, and one who sifts the flour in a sieve, and one who kneads dough, and one who bakes. Additional primary categories of prohibited labor are the following: One who shears wool, and one who whitens it, and one who combs the fleece and straightens it, and one who dyes it, and one who spins the wool, and one who stretches the threads of the warp in the loom, and one who constructs two meshes, tying the threads of the warp to the base of the loom, and one who weaves two threads, and one who severs two threads for constructive purposes, and one who ties a knot, and one who unties a knot, and one who sews two stitches with a needle, as well as one who tears a fabric in order to sew two stitches. One who traps a deer, or any living creature, and one who slaughters it, and one who flays it, and one who salts its hide, a step in the tanning process, and one who tans its hide, and one who smooths it, removing hairs and veins, and one who cuts it into measured parts. One who writes two letters and one who erases in order to write two letters. One who builds a structure, and one who dismantles it, one who extinguishes a fire, and one who kindles a fire. One who strikes a blow with a hammer to complete the production process of a vessel , and one who carries out an object from domain to domain. All these are primary categories of labor, and they number forty-less-one. ", "hebrew_version_title": null, "languageFamilyName": "english", "isPrimary": false}, "highlight": {"naive_lemmatizer": ["This fundamental mishna enumerates those who perform the primary categories of labor prohibited on <b>Shabbat</b>, which number forty-less-one."]}}, {"_index": "text-a", "_id": "Mishneh Torah, Repentance 2:2 (Mishnah Torah, Yod ha-hazakah, trans. by Simon Glazer, 1927 [en])", "_score": 2016362.2, "_source": {"ref": "Mishneh Torah, Repentance 2:2", "heRef": "משנה תורה, הלכות תשובה ב׳:ב׳", "version": "Mishnah Torah, Yod ha-hazakah, trans. by Simon Glazer, 1927", "lang": "en", "version_priority": 1, "titleVariants": ["Mishneh Torah, Laws of Repentance", "Mishneh Torah, Teshuvah", "Rambam, Laws of Repentance", "Rambam, Repentance", "Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuvah", "Rambam, Teshuvah", "Rambam, Hilchot Teshuvah", "Mishneh Torah, Hilkhot Teshuvah", "Rambam, Hilkhot Teshuvah", "Hilkhot Teshuva", "Hilchot Teshuva", "Rambam, Hilkhot Teshuva", "Rambam, Hilchot Teshuva", "Mishneh Torah, Hilkhot Teshuva", "Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Teshuva", "Maimonides, Hilkhoth Teshubah", "Mishneh Torah, Repentance"], "categories": ["Halakhah", "Mishneh Torah", "Sefer Madda"], "order": "A00400000100400020002", "path": "Halakhah/Mishneh Torah/Sefer Madda/Mishneh Torah, Repentance", "pagesheetrank": 246538.31204909337, "comp_date": 1176, "exact": "What is repentance? The sinner shall cease sinning, and remove sin from his thoughts, and wholeheartedly conclude not to revert back to it, even as it is said: \"Let the wicked forsake his way\" ; so, too, shall he be remorseful on what was past, even as it is said: \"Surely after that I was turned, I repented\" . In addition thereto he should take to witness Him Who knoweth all secrets that forever he will not turn to repeat that sin again, according to what it is said: \"Say unto Him.… neither will we call any more the work of our hands our gods\" . It is, moreover, essential that his confession shall be by spoken words of his lips, and all that which he concluded in his heart shall be formed in speech. 2 Yoma, 57b; Shabbat, 121. G.", "naive_lemmatizer": "What is repentance? The sinner shall cease sinning, and remove sin from his thoughts, and wholeheartedly conclude not to revert back to it, even as it is said: \"Let the wicked forsake his way\" ; so, too, shall he be remorseful on what was past, even as it is said: \"Surely after that I was turned, I repented\" . In addition thereto he should take to witness Him Who knoweth all secrets that forever he will not turn to repeat that sin again, according to what it is said: \"Say unto Him.… neither will we call any more the work of our hands our gods\" . It is, moreover, essential that his confession shall be by spoken words of his lips, and all that which he concluded in his heart shall be formed in speech. 2 Yoma, 57b; Shabbat, 121. G.", "hebrew_version_title": "משנה תורה, יד החזקה, תורגם ע״י סיימון גלייזר, 1927", "languageFamilyName": "english", "isPrimary": false}, "highlight": {"naive_lemmatizer": ["It is, moreover, essential that his confession shall be by spoken words of his lips, and all that which he concluded in his heart shall be formed in speech. 2 Yoma, 57b; <b>Shabbat</b>, 121. G."]}}, {"_index": "text-a", "_id": "Shabbat 23b:3 (William Davidson Edition - English [en])", "_score": 1713740.9, "_source": {"ref": "Shabbat 23b:3", "heRef": "שבת כ״ג ב:ג׳", "version": "William Davidson Edition - English", "lang": "en", "version_priority": 0, "titleVariants": ["Masekhet Shabbat", "Shabbos", "Shabbath", "Talmud Shabbat", "Tractate Shabbat", "Shab.", "Sabb.", "Sabbath", "BT Shabbat", "Shabbat"], "categories": ["Talmud", "Bavli", "Seder Moed"], "order": "A00200000100000460003", "path": "Talmud/Bavli/Seder Moed/Shabbat", "pagesheetrank": 155430.38887963092, "comp_date": 450, "exact": " Rava said: It is obvious to me that there is a fixed list of priorities. When a person is poor and must choose between purchasing oil to light a Shabbat lamp for his home or purchasing oil to light a Hanukkah lamp, the Shabbat lamp for his home takes precedence. That is due to peace in his home; without the light of that lamp, his family would be sitting and eating their meal in the dark. Similarly, if there is a conflict between acquiring oil to light a lamp for his home and wine for the sanctification [ kiddush ] of Shabbat day, the lamp for his home takes precedence due to peace in his home. However, Rava raised a dilemma: When the conflict is between oil for a Hanukkah lamp or wine for kiddush of Shabbat day, what is the ruling in that case? Does kiddush of Shabbat day take priority because it is frequent, i.e., it is performed every week, and there is a principle: When there is a conflict between a frequent practice and an infrequent practice, the frequent practice takes precedence? Or, perhaps the Hanukkah lamp takes precedence due to publicity of the miracle? After he raised the dilemma, he then resolved it on his own and he ruled that, in that case, the Hanukkah lamp takes precedence due to publicity of the miracle. ", "naive_lemmatizer": " Rava said: It is obvious to me that there is a fixed list of priorities. When a person is poor and must choose between purchasing oil to light a Shabbat lamp for his home or purchasing oil to light a Hanukkah lamp, the Shabbat lamp for his home takes precedence. That is due to peace in his home; without the light of that lamp, his family would be sitting and eating their meal in the dark. Similarly, if there is a conflict between acquiring oil to light a lamp for his home and wine for the sanctification [ kiddush ] of Shabbat day, the lamp for his home takes precedence due to peace in his home. However, Rava raised a dilemma: When the conflict is between oil for a Hanukkah lamp or wine for kiddush of Shabbat day, what is the ruling in that case? Does kiddush of Shabbat day take priority because it is frequent, i.e., it is performed every week, and there is a principle: When there is a conflict between a frequent practice and an infrequent practice, the frequent practice takes precedence? Or, perhaps the Hanukkah lamp takes precedence due to publicity of the miracle? After he raised the dilemma, he then resolved it on his own and he ruled that, in that case, the Hanukkah lamp takes precedence due to publicity of the miracle. ", "hebrew_version_title": "מהדורת וויליאם דייוידסון - אנגלית", "languageFamilyName": "english", "isPrimary": false}, "highlight": {"naive_lemmatizer": ["When a person is poor and must choose between purchasing oil to light a <b>Shabbat</b> lamp for his home or purchasing oil to light a Hanukkah lamp, the <b>Shabbat</b> lamp for his home takes precedence.", "Similarly, if there is a conflict between acquiring oil to light a lamp for his home and wine for the sanctification [ kiddush ] of <b>Shabbat</b> day, the lamp for his home takes precedence due to peace in his", "However, Rava raised a dilemma: When the conflict is between oil for a Hanukkah lamp or wine for kiddush of <b>Shabbat</b> day, what is the ruling in that case?", "Does kiddush of <b>Shabbat</b> day take priority because it is frequent, i.e., it is performed every week, and there is a principle: When there is a conflict between a frequent practice and an infrequent practice"]}}, {"_index": "text-a", "_id": "Mishneh Torah, Gifts to the Poor 10:7 (Gifts for the Poor, Trans. by Joseph B. Meszler, Williamsburg, Virginia, 2003 [en])", "_score": 1448006.9, "_source": {"ref": "Mishneh Torah, Gifts to the Poor 10:7", "heRef": "משנה תורה, הלכות מתנות עניים י׳:ז׳", "version": "Gifts for the Poor, Trans. by Joseph B. Meszler, Williamsburg, Virginia, 2003", "lang": "en", "version_priority": 1, "titleVariants": ["Rambam, Laws of Gifts to the Poor", "Rambam, Matnot Aniyiim", "Mishneh Torah, Laws of Gifts to the Poor", "Rambam, Gifts to the Poor", "Mishneh Torah, Hilchot Matnot Aniyiim", "Mishneh Torah, Matnot Aniyiim", "Rambam, Hilchot Matnot Aniyiim", "Mishneh Torah, Hilkhot Matnot Aniyiim", "Rambam, Hilkhot Matnot Aniyiim", "Mishneh Torah, Gifts to the Poor"], "categories": ["Halakhah", "Mishneh Torah", "Sefer Zeraim"], "order": "A00400000700100100007", "path": "Halakhah/Mishneh Torah/Sefer Zeraim/Mishneh Torah, Gifts to the Poor", "pagesheetrank": 168728.72308758696, "comp_date": 1176, "exact": "There are eight levels of tzedakah , each one greater than the other. The greatest level, higher than all the rest, is to fortify a fellow Jew and give him a gift, a loan, form with him a partnership, or find work for him, until he is strong enough so that he does not need to ask others [for sustenance]. Of this it is said, [If your kinsman, being in straits, comes under your authority,] and you hold him as though a resident alien, let him live by your side. That is as if to say, \" Hold him up,\" so that he will not fall and be in need. 186 See Babylonian Talmud Shabbat 63a.", "naive_lemmatizer": "There are eight levels of tzedakah , each one greater than the other. The greatest level, higher than all the rest, is to fortify a fellow Jew and give him a gift, a loan, form with him a partnership, or find work for him, until he is strong enough so that he does not need to ask others [for sustenance]. Of this it is said, [If your kinsman, being in straits, comes under your authority,] and you hold him as though a resident alien, let him live by your side. That is as if to say, \" Hold him up,\" so that he will not fall and be in need. 186 See Babylonian Talmud Shabbat 63a.", "hebrew_version_title": null, "languageFamilyName": "english", "isPrimary": false}, "highlight": {"naive_lemmatizer": ["That is as if to say, \" Hold him up,\" so that he will not fall and be in need. 186 See Babylonian Talmud <b>Shabbat</b> 63a."]}}, {"_index": "text-a", "_id": "Shabbat 23b:3 (Sefaria Community Translation [en])", "_score": 1333882.4, "_source": {"ref": "Shabbat 23b:3", "heRef": "שבת כ״ג ב:ג׳", "version": "Sefaria Community Translation", "lang": "en", "version_priority": 1, "titleVariants": ["Masekhet Shabbat", "Shabbos", "Shabbath", "Talmud Shabbat", "Tractate Shabbat", "Shab.", "Sabb.", "Sabbath", "BT Shabbat", "Shabbat"], "categories": ["Talmud", "Bavli", "Seder Moed"], "order": "A00200000100000460003", "path": "Talmud/Bavli/Seder Moed/Shabbat", "pagesheetrank": 155430.38887963092, "comp_date": 450, "exact": "Rav said: It's simple that [if he can afford either] a light for his home [i.e., for Shabbat] and the Chanukkah light, a light for his home is preferred, because of [considerations of] domestic tranquility. A light for his home and [wine for] sanctification of the day, a light for his home is preferred, because of domestic tranquility; Rava asked: The Chanukkah candle and sanctification of the day, what is [the ruling]? Is sanctification of the day preferred, because it is a frequent[practice], or perhaps the Chanukkah candle is prefered, because of publicizing the miracle? After he asked, he came back and resolved [lit. \"simplified\"] it: The Chanukkah candle is preferred, because of publicizing the miracle.", "naive_lemmatizer": "Rav said: It's simple that [if he can afford either] a light for his home [i.e., for Shabbat] and the Chanukkah light, a light for his home is preferred, because of [considerations of] domestic tranquility. A light for his home and [wine for] sanctification of the day, a light for his home is preferred, because of domestic tranquility; Rava asked: The Chanukkah candle and sanctification of the day, what is [the ruling]? Is sanctification of the day preferred, because it is a frequent[practice], or perhaps the Chanukkah candle is prefered, because of publicizing the miracle? After he asked, he came back and resolved [lit. \"simplified\"] it: The Chanukkah candle is preferred, because of publicizing the miracle.", "hebrew_version_title": "תרגום קהילת ספריא", "languageFamilyName": "english", "isPrimary": false}, "highlight": {"naive_lemmatizer": ["Rav said: It's simple that [if he can afford either] a light for his home [i.e., for <b>Shabbat</b>] and the Chanukkah light, a light for his home is preferred, because of [considerations of] domestic tranquility"]}}, {"_index": "text-a", "_id": "Psalms 92:1 (Sefaria Community Translation [en])", "_score": 1256770.9, "_source": {"ref": "Psalms 92:1", "heRef": "תהילים צ״ב:א׳", "version": "Sefaria Community Translation", "lang": "en", "version_priority": 2, "titleVariants": ["Ps", "Ps.", "Psa", "Tehilim", "Psa.", "Psalm", "Tehillim", "Psalms"], "categories": ["Tanakh", "Writings"], "order": "A00000200000920001", "path": "Tanakh/Writings/Psalms", "pagesheetrank": 114561.3330670294, "comp_date": -1000, "exact": "A psalm and song to Shabbat.", "naive_lemmatizer": "A psalm and song to Shabbat.", "hebrew_version_title": "תרגום קהילת ספריא", "languageFamilyName": "english", "isPrimary": false}, "highlight": {"naive_lemmatizer": ["A psalm and song to <b>Shabbat</b>."]}}, {"_index": "text-a", "_id": "Shabbat 21b:1 (William Davidson Edition - English [en])", "_score": 1164730.2, "_source": {"ref": "Shabbat 21b:1", "heRef": "שבת כ״א ב:א׳", "version": "William Davidson Edition - English", "lang": "en", "version_priority": 0, "titleVariants": ["Masekhet Shabbat", "Shabbos", "Shabbath", "Talmud Shabbat", "Tractate Shabbat", "Shab.", "Sabb.", "Sabbath", "BT Shabbat", "Shabbat"], "categories": ["Talmud", "Bavli", "Seder Moed"], "order": "A00200000100000420001", "path": "Talmud/Bavli/Seder Moed/Shabbat", "pagesheetrank": 107099.05882112398, "comp_date": 450, "exact": " one is not bound to attend to it. Therefore, there is no reason to make certain from the outset to light it with materials that burn well, as even if it is extinguished, he is not required to relight it. However, he also holds that it is permitted to use its light. As a result, he must ensure that the wick burns well on Shabbat; if not, he is liable to come to adjust the flame in order to use its light. The third opinion is that which Rabbi Zeira said that Rav Mattana said, and others say that Rabbi Zeira said that Rav said: The wicks and oils with which the Sages said one may not light on Shabbat, one may, nevertheless, light with them on Hanukkah, both during the week and on Shabbat. Rabbi Yirmeya said: What is Rav’s reason? He holds that if it is extinguished, one is not bound to attend to it and relight it, and it is prohibited to use its light. Therefore, even on Shabbat, there is no concern lest he come to adjust the wick, as it is prohibited to utilize its light.", "naive_lemmatizer": " one is not bound to attend to it. Therefore, there is no reason to make certain from the outset to light it with materials that burn well, as even if it is extinguished, he is not required to relight it. However, he also holds that it is permitted to use its light. As a result, he must ensure that the wick burns well on Shabbat; if not, he is liable to come to adjust the flame in order to use its light. The third opinion is that which Rabbi Zeira said that Rav Mattana said, and others say that Rabbi Zeira said that Rav said: The wicks and oils with which the Sages said one may not light on Shabbat, one may, nevertheless, light with them on Hanukkah, both during the week and on Shabbat. Rabbi Yirmeya said: What is Rav’s reason? He holds that if it is extinguished, one is not bound to attend to it and relight it, and it is prohibited to use its light. Therefore, even on Shabbat, there is no concern lest he come to adjust the wick, as it is prohibited to utilize its light.", "hebrew_version_title": "מהדורת וויליאם דייוידסון - אנגלית", "languageFamilyName": "english", "isPrimary": false}, "highlight": {"naive_lemmatizer": ["As a result, he must ensure that the wick burns well on <b>Shabbat</b>; if not, he is liable to come to adjust the flame in order to use its light.", "The third opinion is that which Rabbi Zeira said that Rav Mattana said, and others say that Rabbi Zeira said that Rav said: The wicks and oils with which the Sages said one may not light on <b>Shabbat</b>, one", "may, nevertheless, light with them on Hanukkah, both during the week and on <b>Shabbat</b>.", "Therefore, even on <b>Shabbat</b>, there is no concern lest he come to adjust the wick, as it is prohibited to utilize its light."]}}]}}
2025-08-12 16:46:07,882 - root - DEBUG - formated results: ['Reference: Pirkei Avot 5:6\n Hebrew Reference: משנה אבות ה׳:ו׳\n Highlight: Ten things were created on the eve of the [first] <b>Shabbat</b> at twilight.\n', 'Reference: Mishneh Torah, Repentance 2:1\n Hebrew Reference: משנה תורה, הלכות תשובה ב׳:א׳\n Highlight: Deduct herefrom that if he remembered his Creator and did repent ere he died, he was forgiven. 1 Ibid. 86b; Kiddushin, 40b; <b>Shabbat</b>, 151a. C.\n', 'Reference: Exodus 19:17\n Hebrew Reference: שמות י״ט:י״ז\n Highlight: Seeing this, God had the people retreat and lowered the mountain back to the ground. 79 Rashi on Deuteronomy 32:11. 80 Rashi here and on Deuteronomy 33:2. 81 Rashi on Deuteronomy 33:3 and <b>Shabbat</b> 88a.\n', 'Reference: Mishnah Shabbat 7:2\n Hebrew Reference: משנה שבת ז׳:ב׳\n Highlight: This fundamental mishna enumerates those who perform the primary categories of labor prohibited on <b>Shabbat</b>, which number forty-less-one.\n', 'Reference: Mishneh Torah, Repentance 2:2\n Hebrew Reference: משנה תורה, הלכות תשובה ב׳:ב׳\n Highlight: It is, moreover, essential that his confession shall be by spoken words of his lips, and all that which he concluded in his heart shall be formed in speech. 2 Yoma, 57b; <b>Shabbat</b>, 121. G.\n', 'Reference: Shabbat 23b:3\n Hebrew Reference: שבת כ״ג ב:ג׳\n Highlight: When a person is poor and must choose between purchasing oil to light a <b>Shabbat</b> lamp for his home or purchasing oil to light a Hanukkah lamp, the <b>Shabbat</b> lamp for his home takes precedence. [...] Similarly, if there is a conflict between acquiring oil to light a lamp for his home and wine for the sanctification [ kiddush ] of <b>Shabbat</b> day, the lamp for his home takes precedence due to peace in his [...] However, Rava raised a dilemma: When the conflict is between oil for a Hanukkah lamp or wine for kiddush of <b>Shabbat</b> day, what is the ruling in that case? [...] Does kiddush of <b>Shabbat</b> day take priority because it is frequent, i.e., it is performed every week, and there is a principle: When there is a conflict between a frequent practice and an infrequent practice\n', 'Reference: Mishneh Torah, Gifts to the Poor 10:7\n Hebrew Reference: משנה תורה, הלכות מתנות עניים י׳:ז׳\n Highlight: That is as if to say, " Hold him up," so that he will not fall and be in need. 186 See Babylonian Talmud <b>Shabbat</b> 63a.\n', "Reference: Shabbat 23b:3\n Hebrew Reference: שבת כ״ג ב:ג׳\n Highlight: Rav said: It's simple that [if he can afford either] a light for his home [i.e., for <b>Shabbat</b>] and the Chanukkah light, a light for his home is preferred, because of [considerations of] domestic tranquility\n", 'Reference: Psalms 92:1\n Hebrew Reference: תהילים צ״ב:א׳\n Highlight: A psalm and song to <b>Shabbat</b>.\n', 'Reference: Shabbat 21b:1\n Hebrew Reference: שבת כ״א ב:א׳\n Highlight: As a result, he must ensure that the wick burns well on <b>Shabbat</b>; if not, he is liable to come to adjust the flame in order to use its light. [...] The third opinion is that which Rabbi Zeira said that Rav Mattana said, and others say that Rabbi Zeira said that Rav said: The wicks and oils with which the Sages said one may not light on <b>Shabbat</b>, one [...] may, nevertheless, light with them on Hanukkah, both during the week and on <b>Shabbat</b>. [...] Therefore, even on <b>Shabbat</b>, there is no concern lest he come to adjust the wick, as it is prohibited to utilize its light.\n']
